Implement the following four changes. Each is independent, but they touch the same role/permission logic, so keep that logic in one shared place rather than duplicating checks per feature.
	1.	Dashboard widget visibility and role scoped refresh
For every widget on the dashboard that is backed by a job, check the current user's role access to that job using the same permission check already used to filter the job list on the Jobs view. If the user's role does not have access to that job, do not render the widget at all for that user. No placeholder, no error state, no empty box, it simply should not appear.
Keep the manual refresh control, but scope what it triggers to only the widgets currently visible to that user. An admin sees every widget, so their refresh triggers every widget. A developer or tester sees only the widgets tied to jobs their role can access, so their refresh only triggers those. The refresh action must never be able to trigger a job the current user's role lacks access to, and this must be enforced in the backend endpoint the refresh call hits, not only by hiding the button in the frontend, since a hidden button does not stop a direct call to the endpoint.
	2.	Console view job filtering and cleanup of a duplicate entry
Apply the exact same role based job access filter already used on the Jobs view to the Console view's job list, so each user only sees the jobs their role has access to, instead of seeing every job and getting a job not found error when they lack access to one they can see.
Separately, there is a cleanup item: a job was migrated from the Jobs view to the Console view in an earlier change, but it was never removed from the Jobs view, so it currently still appears in both places. Find that job and remove it from the Jobs view so it only exists in the Console view going forward.
	3.	Move user accounts from the existing config file into SQLite, with a safe first run
Move user account storage, meaning username, password hash, and role, out of the existing role and access configuration file and into a SQLite database file. Store this database file at a path outside the application's deployed and version controlled code directory, using the same kind of persistent external data path already used for job history and log storage, so that code deployments, git pulls, and restarts never touch or overwrite it.
Passwords must be stored only as salted hashes, using the standard library's pbkdf2_hmac or an equivalent already available without adding a new dependency. Never store a plaintext or reversibly encrypted password. Authentication should hash the entered password with the stored salt and compare it against the stored hash.
On startup, if the database file does not already exist, create it and seed exactly one bootstrap admin account with the admin role, using a username and password read from environment variables rather than hardcoded in source code. Log a clear message that a bootstrap admin account was created on first run. On every subsequent startup, if the database file already exists, skip this bootstrap step entirely and load accounts as they are.
Keep the three existing roles, admin, developer, and tester. Update the existing per job role checks and the existing Jobs view filtering logic so they read role information from this new SQLite store instead of the old configuration file.
	4.	Admin only account management view
Add a new entry point that is visible only to users whose role is admin, kept separate from the existing Dashboard, Jobs, and Console views. The recommended treatment is a small settings icon in the header that only renders for admin role users, opening a dedicated admin view, rather than adding a fourth peer level tab.
Inside this admin only view, provide the ability to add a new user with a username, an initial password, and a role chosen from admin, developer, or tester, remove an existing user, reset an existing user's password to a new value, and change an existing user's assigned role after the fact. All of these actions must write directly to the SQLite store from item 3 and take effect immediately, without requiring an application restart.
None of these actions should be reachable by a non admin user. Enforce this both by hiding the entry point in the frontend and by checking the caller's role on the backend endpoints that perform these actions, since the frontend check alone is not sufficient.
